Abstract classes.

Sometimes a base class is a genuine concept — every subtype really is one — but the base itself is too general to actually create. What is "a shape", if you have no idea whether it's a circle or a square? An abstract class captures exactly that: a partial class you can extend, but never create directly with new.

You mark a class abstract, and the compiler then forbids new on it. It exists only to be extended. So an abstract shape class might declare an area method with no body at all, and also a concrete describe method, with a body, that all shapes share. If you try to write new shape, that's a compile error, because shape is abstract.

That area method, with a signature and no body, is an abstract method. It's a promise, not an implementation — a contract that says "every concrete subclass must provide this." A subclass that doesn't implement every abstract method is itself still abstract. But fill them all in, and the subclass becomes concrete, meaning you can finally create it. So a circle extends shape, stores a radius, and overrides area to return pi times radius squared, fulfilling the contract. Now new circle works. And describe, which the circle inherited unchanged, calls the circle's own area through dynamic dispatch. That's the pattern: the base defines the shared skeleton and the holes, and each subclass fills the holes.

Here's where it gets powerful. Because an abstract class can mix concrete methods with abstract ones, it can lay out an algorithm's structure, while leaving specific steps to the subclasses. Picture an abstract report class with a print method that calls header, then body, then footer, in that fixed order. Header and footer have shared default bodies. Body is abstract — each kind of report fills it in. And you can even mark print as final, so no subclass can change the ordering. Subclasses supply only the body; the sequence and the shared parts are guaranteed. That's real reuse of behaviour, not just of fields.

So when do you choose an abstract class over an interface? Both let you program to an abstraction, and the line between them narrowed once interfaces gained default methods — but the distinction still stands. Reach for an abstract class when your subtypes share state, that is fields, and implementation, and there's a real is-a hierarchy; remember a class can extend only one. Reach for an interface, which is the next section, when you're defining a capability that unrelated types can implement, and you want a type to mix in several of them. The rule of thumb: an abstract class for a partial thing, an interface for a capability. And when in doubt, prefer the interface, because it couples less — which is exactly where we head next.
